{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01U9Aoz5uG9BhTjbzKUm2YTL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UVPJeteNu9kkU463iUdhXw","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Limits of Post-Hoc Contamination Calibration: An IRT-Based Diagnostic Study\"\n\n**Core idea:** The paper proposes ContamCal, a two-stage framework that (1) maps item-level contamination provenance via RRF of 4 detection signals, then (2) fits a 4PL IRT model augmented with per-benchmark contamination parameter γ_k. The paper frames itself as a **negative results** paper — it finds IRT-based calibration fails systematically for structural reasons.\n\n**Three claimed contributions (all negative):**\n1. Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent and non-monotonic (ARC catastrophic forgetting, GSM8K resistant, MMLU negligible at scale)\n2. Signal failure mode characterization: only embedding similarity works at 0.5B; Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d=-1.98); all signals fail on RL models\n3. Identifiability impossibility: γ unidentifiable when N<50 and signal AUC<0.80 (typical LLM evaluation conditions)\n\n**Related work covered in the paper (Section 2):**\n- Benchmark contamination detection: Min-K++ (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024), membership inference (Shokri et al., 2017; Carlini et al., 2022)\n- IRT for LLM evaluation: PSN-IRT (Polo et al., 2024), Growing Pains (Burnell et al., 2023), ATLAS, Lalor et al. (2024). None use contamination as covariate.\n- Contamination mitigation: DCR (Oren et al., 2024), Xu et al. (2024) showing 20 strategies fail, LiveBench (White et al., 2024), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al., 2024), HiBayES, HELM (Liang et al., 2023)\n- Calibration/uncertainty: Distinguished from probability calibration (Guo et al., 2017; Platt, 1999); conformal prediction (Angelopoulos & Bates, 2023)\n- DIF: Holland & Wainer (1993), Thissen et al. (1993), Zumbo (1999). Paper notes key difference: group membership is continuous latent variable, not discrete.\n\n**Key paper details:**\n- 12 pages total (10 main + 2 references)\n- Targets EMNLP (mentioned in intro: \"diagnostic study relevant to EMNLP's mission\")\n- No appendix/supplementary material visible\n- All code/checkpoints/data promised upon publication\n- Experiments: Qwen2.5-{0.5B,1.5B,3B,7B}, Llama-3.2-1B, observational on Instruct/DeepSeek-R1, 72B inference-only\n- Stan NUTS for Bayesian estimation\n\n**The experimental design context shows these experiments were imagined:**\n- exp-001: Controlled SFT injection (covered in Tables 1-2)\n- exp-002: Stage 1 signal pipeline (Tables 4-5)\n- exp-003: RRF aggregation ablation (Table 6)\n- exp-004: Simulation identifiability sweep (Tables 8-9)\n- exp-005: Noise propagation (Table 12)\n- exp-006: IRT calibration + baselines (Tables 10-11)\n- exp-007: 7B scale bridging (Table 2)\n- exp-008: Signal survival analysis (Tables 4-5, 7)\n- exp-009: RL observational (Tables 7, 13)\n- exp-012: 72B observational (text mention)\n- exp-013: Closed-weight 2-signal (Table 6, text)\n- exp-014: Multi-family validation (Table 3)\n- exp-015: QC-only ablation (text)\n\nAll experiments appear covered in the paper, though some are compressed into brief text mentions rather than full table presentations.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the three negative contributions? Are they surprising to the community? Would a careful researcher have predicted these outcomes? Is the Min-K++ reversal genuinely new knowledge?\n\n2. **Significance/Impact**: Would this change how practitioners approach contamination? Is the \"impossibility result\" impactful? Who benefits from this paper?\n\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing references? Is the DIF connection properly developed? Is the IRT literature coverage adequate? Are there recent contamination papers missing?\n\n4. **Positioning and framing**: Is the negative-results framing effective? Does the paper oversell or undersell its contributions? Is the EMNLP targeting appropriate?\n\n5. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Are the contributions clearly stated? Is the notation consistent?\n\n6. **Comparison with alternatives**: Does the paper adequately explain why simpler approaches (e.g., linear regression) might suffice? Does it position itself fairly against LiveBench/dynamic benchmarks?\n\n7. **Community value**: Is this the kind of negative result that advances the field, or is it predictable? Does it open new research directions?\n\n## Output Format\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Minor Issues** (bulleted)\n- **Preliminary Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Negative-results papers at top venues must demonstrate that the negative finding is genuinely surprising and useful — not merely confirming what experts would expect."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15658,"cache_read_input_tokens":47935,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15658},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e2bc174-5249-4ea4-a7dc-2e60f9f53d62","uuid":"aa3545bc-a8a3-4411-8e8b-046aac2f94d5"}
