here missing references? Is the DIF connection properly developed? Is the IRT literature coverage adequate? Are there recent contamination papers missing?\n\n4. **Positioning and framing**: Is the negative-results framing effective? Does the paper oversell or undersell its contributions? Is the EMNLP targeting appropriate?\n\n5. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Are the contributions clearly stated? Is the notation consistent?\n\n6. **Comparison with alternatives**: Does the paper adequately explain why simpler approaches (e.g., linear regression) might suffice? Does it position itself fairly against LiveBench/dynamic benchmarks?\n\n7. **Community value**: Is this the kind of negative result that advances the field, or is it predictable? Does it open new research directions?\n\n## Output Format\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Minor Issues** (bulleted)\n- **Preliminary Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Negative-results papers at top venues must demonstrate that the negative finding is genuinely surprising and useful — not merely confirming what experts would expect.","uuid":"dcfe8d19-7531-4757-8f15-271e73e48285","session_id":"2e2bc174-5249-4ea4-a7dc-2e60f9f53d62"}
