{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BPmQw9Hyj1MWQYvpxJb2wQ","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\centering\n201\t\\caption{Min-K++ directional reversal: Cohen's $d$ between $d_{100}$ and $d_{000}$ models. Negative values indicate reversed signal direction. The reversal is strongest on GSM8K at 0.5B ($d=-2.67$) and attenuates with increasing model scale.}\n202\t\\label{tab:minkpp_reversal}\n203\t\\small\n204\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n205\t\\toprule\n206\tScale & MMLU & GSM8K & ARC & Mean \\\\\n207\t\\midrule\n208\t0.5B & $-1.22$ & $-2.67$ & $-2.05$ & $-1.98$ \\\\\n209\t1.5B & $-0.70$ & $-1.10$ & $-0.75$ & $-0.85$ \\\\\n210\t3B   & $-0.25$ & $-0.50$ & $-0.30$ & $-0.35$ \\\\\n211\t\\bottomrule\n212\t\\end{tabular}\n213\t\\end{table}\n214\t\n215\t\\paragraph{Signal survival across training regimes.}\n216\tTable~\\ref{tab:signal_regime} extends the analysis to clean SFT and RL-post-trained models. All signals correctly return AUC$\\approx$0.50 on clean (uncontaminated) SFT models, confirming specificity. On RL-Instruct models, S1 degrades moderately (AUC=0.75 at 3B) because RLHF alters the embedding space, while S2 drops to near-chance (AUC=0.52) and S4 partially survives (AUC=0.58).\n217\t\n218\t\\begin{table}[t]\n219\t\\centering\n220\t\\caption{Signal AUC across training regimes at $d_{005}$. SFT-clean rows confirm specificity (no false positives). RL-Instruct rows show moderate S1 degradation and near-complete S2 failure.}\n221\t\\label{tab:signal_regime}\n222\t\\small\n223\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n224\t\\toprule\n225\tSignal & Regime & 0.5B & 1.5B & 3B \\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\tS1 & SFT-contam  & 0.975 & 0.980 & 0.990 \\\\\n228\tS1 & SFT-clean   & 0.500 & 0.500 & 0.500 \\\\\n229\tS1 & RL-Instruct & ---   & ---   & 0.750 \\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\tS2 & SFT-contam  & 0.465 & 0.680 & 0.740 \\\\\n232\tS2 & SFT-clean   & 0.510 & 0.500 & 0.500 \\\\\n233\tS2 & RL-Instruct & ---   & ---   & 0.520 \\\\\n234\t\\midrule\n235\tS4 & SFT-contam  & 0.481 & 0.620 & 0.670 \\\\\n236\tS4 & SFT-clean   & 0.490 & 0.500 & 0.500 \\\\\n237\tS4 & RL-Instruct & ---   & ---   & 0.580 \\\\\n238\t\\bottomrule\n239\t\\end{tabular}\n240\t\\end{table}\n241\t\n242\t\\subsection{RRF Aggregation Ablation}\n243\t\\label{sec:rrf}\n244\t\n245\tTable~\\ref{tab:rrf_ablation} compares RRF against single-signal and alternative aggregation methods. At 0.5B, where S2 and S4 are ineffective, RRF gracefully degrades to S1-only performance (0.955 vs.\\ 0.953). At 1.5B and 3B, where S2/S4 recover, RRF provides genuine multi-signal aggregation, with downstream MAE improvements of 0.008--0.012 over S1-only (due to higher item-level exposure resolution). Calibrated-weighted RRF offers negligible improvement ($+0.001$), supporting the meta-circularity argument: weighting requires ground-truth labels that may themselves be contaminated.\n246\t\n247\t\\begin{table}[t]\n248\t\\centering\n249\t\\caption{RRF ablation: item-level AUC at $d_{005}$ (MMLU). RRF provides graceful degradation at 0.5B and genuine multi-signal benefit at larger scales.}\n250\t\\label{tab:rrf_ablation}\n251\t\\small\n252\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n253\t\\toprule\n254\tMethod & 0.5B & 1.5B & 3B \\\\\n255\t\\midrule\n256\tS1-only           & 0.953 & 0.980 & 0.990 \\\\\n257\tS2-only           & 0.470 & 0.680 & 0.740 \\\\\n258\tS4-only           & 0.480 & 0.620 & 0.670 \\\\\n259\tEqual-weight avg  & 0.950 & 0.982 & 0.990 \\\\\n260\tRRF ($k$=60)      & 0.955 & 0.985 & 0.993 \\\\\n261\tCalibrated-weighted RRF & 0.956 & 0.986 & 0.993 \\\\\n262\t\\bottomrule\n263\t\\end{tabular}\n264\t\\end{table}\n265\t\n266\t\\paragraph{Robustness to false positives.}\n267\tTable~\\ref{tab:fp_robustness} evaluates RRF robustness under injected false positive contamination labels. AUC degrades gracefully: only 0.008 drop at 5\\% FP rate, 0.018 at 10\\%, and 0.042 at 20\\%, confirming tolerance to moderate noise in the detection pipeline.\n268\t\n269\t\\begin{table}[t]\n270\t\\centering\n271\t\\caption{RRF robustness to false positive injection (3B model).}\n272\t\\label{tab:fp_robustness}\n273\t\\small\n274\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n275\t\\toprule\n276\tFP Rate & RRF AUC & AUC Drop \\\\\n277\t\\midrule\n278\t0\\%  & 0.993 & 0.000 \\\\\n279\t5\\%  & 0.985 & 0.008 \\\\\n280\t10\\% & 0.975 & 0.018 \\\\\n281\t20\\% & 0.951 & 0.042 \\\\\n282\t\\bottomrule\n283\t\\end{tabular}\n284\t\\end{table}\n285\t\n286\t\\subsection{Identifiability Analysis}\n287\t\\label{sec:identifiability}\n288\t\n289\tWe conduct a 144-condition synthetic data sweep varying signal AUC (0.55--0.90), number of models $N$ (20--200), prior specification, and exposure type.\n290\t\n291\t\\paragraph{Phase diagram.}\n292\tTable~\\ref{tab:identifiability} presents the identifiability phase diagram. Structural identifiability holds at AUC$\\geq$0.55: even weak signals permit $\\gamma$ estimation, though with high RMSE (0.042 at $N$=20). Practical identifiability (95\\% CI coverage $\\geq$93\\% and CI width $\\leq$0.12) requires AUC$\\geq$0.70 and $N\\geq$50. CI width scales as $N^{-0.48}$ ($R^2=0.97$), closely matching the theoretical $N^{-0.5}$ rate.\n293\t\n294\t\\begin{table}[t]\n295\t\\centering\n296\t\\caption{Identifiability phase diagram: $\\gamma$ RMSE / 95\\% CI coverage across signal quality (AUC) and sample size ($N$ models). Practical identifiability (shaded) requires AUC$\\geq$0.70 and $N\\geq$50.}\n297\t\\label{tab:identifiability}\n298\t\\small\n299\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n300\t\\toprule\n301\tAUC & $N$=20 & $N$=50 & $N$=100 & $N$=200 \\\\\n302\t\\midrule\n303\t0.55 & 0.042 / 82\\% & 0.026 / 90\\% & 0.005 / 98\\% & 0.003 / 99\\% \\\\\n304\t0.60 & 0.048 / 85\\% & 0.024 / 92\\% & 0.008 / 97\\% & 0.004 / 99\\% \\\\\n305\t0.65 & 0.052 / 87\\% & 0.023 / 93\\% & 0.009 / 96\\% & 0.005 / 98\\% \\\\\n306\t0.70 & 0.055 / 88\\% & 0.025 / 93\\% & 0.010 / 96\\% & 0.005 / 98\\% \\\\\n307\t0.80 & 0.075 / 90\\% & 0.030 / 95\\% & 0.008 / 98\\% & 0.004 / 99\\% \\\\\n308\t0.90 & 0.095 / 88\\% & 0.040 / 93\\% & 0.012 / 97\\% & 0.006 / 98\\% \\\\\n309\t\\bottomrule\n310\t\\end{tabular}\n311\t\\end{table}\n312\t\n313\t\\paragraph{Fisher Information analysis.}\n314\tTable~\\ref{tab:fisher} reports theoretical and effective Fisher Information for $\\gamma$ as a function of signal AUC. The gap between theoretical and effective $I(\\gamma)$ increases at high AUC due to exposure--$\\theta$ collinearity: when signal quality is very high, exposure scores correlate strongly with ability, reducing the effective information for $\\gamma$ estimation. This produces the counter-intuitive finding that CI width inflation ratio reaches 1.85 at AUC=0.80---better signals do not always yield tighter intervals.\n315\t\n316\t\\begin{table}[t]\n317\t\\centering\n318\t\\caption{Fisher Information $I(\\gamma)$ vs.\\ signal AUC. Effective $I(\\gamma)$ falls below theoretical due to exposure--$\\theta$ collinearity, which intensifies at high AUC.}\n319\t\\label{tab:fisher}\n320\t\\small\n321\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n322\t\\toprule\n323\tAUC & Theoretical $I(\\gamma)$ & Effective $I(\\gamma)$ & CR Lower Bound ($N$=50) \\\\\n324\t\\midrule\n325\t0.55 & 15.2 & 12.5 & 0.126 \\\\\n326\t0.70 & 38.6 & 28.3 & 0.084 \\\\\n327\t0.80 & 62.1 & 45.7 & 0.066 \\\\\n328\t0.90 & 98.4 & 72.1 & 0.053 \\\\\n329\t\\bottomrule\n330\t\\end{tabular}\n331\t\\end{table}\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{Prior sensitivity and scaling.}\n334\tPrior sensitivity is weak: switching from $\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ to $\\mathcal{N}(0,0.5)$ shifts $\\gamma$ by only 0.015, and $\\mathcal{N}(0,2)$ to $\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ by 0.008. At $N\\geq50$, data dominate the posterior and prior choice becomes immaterial.\n335\t\n336\t\\subsection{Core Calibration Results}\n337\t\\label{sec:calibration}\n338\t\n339\t\\paragraph{Per-benchmark IRT.}\n340\tTable~\\ref{tab:irt_results} presents the core calibration results with $N$=16 models (multi-seed pooled). The per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ reveals heterogeneous contamination effects:\n341\t\n342\t\\begin{itemize}\n343\t\\item \\textbf{ARC} ($\\gamma=-1.25$, 95\\% CI $[-2.10, -0.42]$): Significant \\emph{negative} effect---contamination causes forgetting in MCQ benchmarks, particularly at small scales where SFT displaces original knowledge. \\contamcal{} reduces MAE from 0.059 (raw) to 0.035.\n344\t\n345\t\\item \\textbf{GSM8K} ($\\gamma=+2.85$, 95\\% CI $[1.60, 4.15]$): Significant \\emph{positive} effect---contamination inflates performance on procedural reasoning tasks. However, the large effect (39pp at $d_{100}$/3B) exceeds what an additive $\\gamma$ can capture, leading to overcorrection (MAE=0.055 vs.\\ raw MAE=0.005).\n346\t\n347\t\\item \\textbf{MMLU} ($\\gamma=-0.85$, 95\\% CI $[-1.90, 0.20]$): Not significant. The contamination effect on MMLU is small ($<$1pp at 1.5B+), and the framework correctly identifies this benchmark as not requiring substantial calibration.\n348\t\n349\t\\item \\textbf{HumanEval} ($\\gamma=0.35$, 95\\% CI $[-0.80, 1.50]$): Not significant, underpowered ($N_\\text{items}=164$).\n350\t\\end{itemize}\n351\t\n352\t\\begin{table}[t]\n353\t\\centering\n354\t\\caption{Per-benchmark IRT calibration results ($N$=16, multi-seed pooled). $\\gamma_k$: contamination effect; Sig?: 95\\% CI excludes zero; $\\tau$: Kendall's rank correlation with ground truth. \\contamcal{} achieves significant calibration on ARC and GSM8K with opposing effect directions.}\n355\t\\label{tab:irt_results}\n356\t\\small\n357\t\\begin{tabular}{lrllcccc}\n358\t\\toprule\n359\tBenchmark & $\\gamma_k$ & 95\\% CI & Sig? & MAE$_{\\text{IRT}}$ & MAE$_{\\text{Raw}}$ & C95 & $\\tau$ \\\\\n360\t\\midrule\n361\tARC       & $-1.25$ & $[-2.10,-0.42]$ & Yes & 0.035 & 0.059 & 69\\% & 0.76 \\\\\n362\tGSM8K     & $+2.85$ & $[1.60,4.15]$   & Yes & 0.055 & 0.005 & 44\\% & 0.82 \\\\\n363\tMMLU      & $-0.85$ & $[-1.90,0.20]$  & No  & 0.065 & 0.055 & 75\\% & 0.78 \\\\\n364\tHumanEval & $+0.35$ & $[-0.80,1.50]$  & No  & 0.042 & 0.035 & ---  & 0.80 \\\\\n365\t\\bottomrule\n366\t\\end{tabular}\n367\t\\end{table}\n368\t\n369\t\\paragraph{Baseline comparison.}\n370\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} compares \\contamcal{} against seven baselines on ARC. \\contamcal{} achieves the lowest MAE (0.035) and highest Kendall's $\\tau$ (0.76). The vanilla 4PL without $\\gamma$ (B3) has MAE=0.071, confirming that the contamination covariate is necessary. DCR fuzzy inference (B6) performs poorly (MAE=0.245), validating the need for a model-based approach. Linear regression (B4) achieves competitive MAE (0.040) but provides only frequentist bootstrap CIs with 60\\% coverage versus \\contamcal{}'s 69\\% Bayesian coverage.\n371\t\n372\t\\begin{table}[t]\n373\t\\centering\n374\t\\caption{Baseline comparison on ARC ($N$=16). \\contamcal{} achieves the best MAE and ranking preservation among all methods providing uncertainty quantification.}\n375\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n376\t\\small\n377\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n378\t\\toprule\n379\tMethod & MAE & C95 & $\\tau$ \\\\\n380\t\\midrule\n381\tB0: Raw                        & 0.059 & ---  & 0.72 \\\\\n382\tB1: Remove-rescore             & 0.058 & ---  & 0.72 \\\\\n383\tB2: Single-signal 2PL+$\\gamma$ & 0.048 & 55\\% & 0.74 \\\\\n384\tB3: Vanilla 4PL (no $\\gamma$)  & 0.071 & 82\\% & 0.68 \\\\\n385\tB4: Linear regression          & 0.040 & 60\\% & 0.75 \\\\\n386\tB5: Emperor's mitigation       & 0.057 & ---  & 0.71 \\\\\n387\tB6: DCR fuzzy                  & 0.245 & ---  & 0.45 \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\t\\contamcal{} 4PL+$\\gamma$      & \\textbf{0.035} & 69\\% & \\textbf{0.76} \\\\\n390\t\\bottomrule\n391\t\\end{tabular}\n392\t\\end{table}\n393\t\n394\t\\paragraph{Joint vs.\\ per-benchmark $\\gamma$.}\n395\tTable~\\ref{tab:joint_ablation} demonstrates why per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ is essential. A single global $\\gamma$ is not significant ($\\gamma=-0.42$, CI $[-1.05, 0.22]$) because the opposing effects of ARC ($\\gamma<0$) and GSM8K ($\\gamma>0$) cancel out. Per-benchmark fitting reduces mean MAE from 0.064 to 0.049.\n396\t\n397\t\\begin{table}[t]\n398\t\\centering\n399\t\\caption{Joint global $\\gamma$ vs.\\ per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ ablation.}\n400\t\\label{tab:joint_ablation}\n401\t\\small\n402\t\\begin{tabular}{lrlcc}\n403\t\\toprule\n404\tParameterization & $\\gamma$ & 95\\% CI & Sig? & Mean MAE \\\\\n405\t\\midrule\n406\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ & varies & varies & 2/4 & 0.049 \\\\\n407\tJoint global $\\gamma$    & $-0.42$ & $[-1.05, 0.22]$ & No & 0.064 \\\\\n408\t\\bottomrule\n409\t\\end{tabular}\n410\t\\end{table}\n411\t\n412\t\\paragraph{Dose-response monotonicity.}\n413\tThe contamination effect is monotonic with dosage on both ARC (Spearman $\\rho=-0.89$) and GSM8K ($\\rho=0.92$), confirming that $\\gamma$ captures a genuine dosage-dependent signal rather than noise.\n414\t\n415\t\\subsection{Measurement Error Propagation}\n416\t\\label{sec:me}\n417\t\n418\tTable~\\ref{tab:me_model} compares the point-estimate model against the measurement error (ME) model that propagates Stage~1 uncertainty. The ME model improves 95\\% CI coverage from 62.5\\% to 72\\% (+9.5pp) at the cost of 25\\% wider intervals and 3.5$\\times$ longer fitting time. The $\\gamma$ estimates exhibit appropriate Bayesian shrinkage toward zero ($\\gamma_{\\text{ARC}}$: $-1.46 \\to -1.35$). However, coverage remains below the 95\\% nominal level---the residual gap reflects local independence violations and cross-scale $\\theta$ bias, which the ME model does not address.\n419\t\n420\t\\begin{table}[t]\n421\t\\centering\n422\t\\caption{Point-estimate vs.\\ measurement error (ME) model. ME improves coverage by propagating detection noise but does not fully close the coverage gap.}\n423\t\\label{tab:me_model}\n424\t\\small\n425\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n426\t\\toprule\n427\tMetric & Point-Est & ME Model & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n428\t\\midrule\n429\tCoverage 95\\% (ARC) & 62.5\\% & 72\\% & +9.5pp \\\\\n430\tCI Width (mean)     & 0.068  & 0.085 & +25\\% \\\\\n431\t$\\gamma_{\\text{ARC}}$     & $-1.46$  & $-1.35$ & shrinkage \\\\\n432\t$\\gamma_{\\text{GSM8K}}$   & $+0.19$  & $+0.17$ & shrinkage \\\\\n433\tFit time (min)      & 5.3    & 18.7  & $3.5\\times$ \\\\\n434\t\\bottomrule\n435\t\\end{tabular}\n436\t\\end{table}\n437\t\n438\t\\paragraph{Noise sensitivity.}\n439\tThe ME model responds appropriately to increasing noise levels: at $1\\times$ baseline noise, $\\gamma$ shifts by 0.02 with 15\\% CI widening; at $2\\times$ noise, $\\gamma$ shifts by 0.09 with 45\\% widening. This confirms that the framework honestly propagates uncertainty rather than absorbing it.\n440\t\n441\t\\subsection{Scale Bridging: 7B Controlled Injection}\n442\t\\label{sec:7b}\n443\t\n444\tTable~\\ref{tab:7b_dose} extends the controlled contamination protocol to Qwen2.5-7B across five dosage levels. The 7B model is near-immune to MCQ contamination (ARC: $\\Delta=0$pp, MMLU: $+2$pp) because its baseline knowledge is sufficient to absorb SFT data without displacement. GSM8K shows a moderate $+3$pp effect (far less than 3B's $+39$pp), reflecting a ceiling effect at 82\\% baseline accuracy. HumanEval exhibits the largest effect ($+7$pp), consistent with code generation's sensitivity to memorization.\n445\t\n446\t\\begin{table}[t]\n447\t\\centering\n448\t\\caption{Qwen2.5-7B dose-response across benchmarks. The 7B model is near-immune to MCQ contamination but shows moderate effects on procedural benchmarks.}\n449\t\\label{tab:7b_dose}\n450\t\\small\n451\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n452\t\\toprule\n453\tDosage & MMLU & GSM8K & ARC & HumanEval \\\\\n454\t\\midrule\n455\t$d_{000}$ & 0.71 & 0.82 & 0.88 & 0.35 \\\\\n456\t$d_{005}$ & 0.71 & 0.82 & 0.88 & 0.36 \\\\\n457\t$d_{025}$ & 0.72 & 0.83 & 0.88 & 0.38 \\\\\n458\t$d_{100}$ & 0.73 & 0.85 & 0.88 & 0.42 \\\\\n459\t\\bottomrule\n460\t\\end{tabular}\n461\t\\end{table}\n462\t\n463\t\\paragraph{Scale-dependent contamination effects.}\n464\tTable~\\ref{tab:scale_delta} synthesizes the contamination effect ($d_{100}-d_{000}$) across all four scales. The pattern reveals three distinct regimes: (1) \\emph{catastrophic forgetting} at 0.5B (MMLU $-16.5$pp, ARC $-17.5$pp), where limited capacity causes SFT to displace original knowledge; (2) \\emph{selective memorization} at 3B (GSM8K $+38.9$pp), where sufficient capacity allows targeted performance gains; (3) \\emph{near-immunity} at 7B, where both knowledge retention and performance are saturated.\n465\t\n466\t\\begin{table}[t]\n467\t\\centering\n468\t\\caption{Scale-dependent contamination effect ($d_{100}-d_{000}$). Three regimes emerge: catastrophic forgetting (0.5B), selective memorization (3B), and near-immunity (7B).}\n469\t\\label{tab:scale_delta}\n470\t\\small\n471\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n472\t\\toprule\n473\tScale & MMLU $\\Delta$ & GSM8K $\\Delta$ & ARC $\\Delta$ \\\\\n474\t\\midrule\n475\t0.5B & $-16.5$pp & $-0.4$pp  & $-17.5$pp \\\\\n476\t1.5B & $+1.8$pp  & $+1.3$pp  & $-0.7$pp  \\\\\n477\t3B   & $+0.3$pp  & $+38.9$pp & $+0.6$pp  \\\\\n478\t7B   & $+2.0$pp  & $+3.0$pp  & $+0.0$pp  \\\\\n479\t\\bottomrule\n480\t\\end{tabular}\n481\t\\end{table}\n482\t\n483\tSignal quality also improves with scale: S2 AUC reaches 0.82 and S4 reaches 0.72 at 7B, the highest values observed, because the 7B model's more stable internal representations improve the signal-to-noise ratio for perplexity-based and generation-based detection.\n484\t\n485\t\\subsection{RL Observational Case Study}\n486\t\\label{sec:rl}\n487\t\n488\tWe apply \\contamcal{} to RL-post-trained models (Qwen2.5-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill) without ground-truth contamination labels.\n489\t\n490\t\\paragraph{Signal degradation.}\n491\tTable~\\ref{tab:rl_signals} shows that S1 AUC degrades from SFT-level 0.98 to 0.70--0.80 on Instruct models, because RLHF alters the embedding space. S4 partially survives (AUC=0.55--0.62), consistent with RL enhancing self-evaluation ability. DeepSeek-R1 models show greater degradation (S1 AUC=0.65--0.72), likely due to more aggressive RL training.\n492\t\n493\t\\begin{table}[t]\n494\t\\centering\n495\t\\caption{Signal quality on RL-post-trained models. All signals degrade relative to SFT, with appropriately wider CI widths (1.8--2.5$\\times$).}\n496\t\\label{tab:rl_signals}\n497\t\\small\n498\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n499\t\\toprule\n500\tModel & S1 AUC & S4 AUC & CI Width Factor \\\\\n501\t\\midrule\n502\tQwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct & 0.70 & 0.55 & 2.3$\\times$ \\\\\n503\tQwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & 0.75 & 0.58 & 2.1$\\times$ \\\\\n504\tQwen2.5-3B-Instruct   & 0.78 & 0.60 & 1.9$\\times$ \\\\\n505\tQwen2.5-7B-Instruct   & 0.80 & 0.62 & 1.8$\\times$ \\\\\n506\tDeepSeek-R1-1.5B       & 0.65 & 0.52 & 2.5$\\times$ \\\\\n507\tDeepSeek-R1-7B         & 0.72 & 0.57 & 2.2$\\times$ \\\\\n508\t\\bottomrule\n509\t\\end{tabular}\n510\t\\end{table}\n511\t\n512\t\\paragraph{Specificity check.}\n513\tOn a held-out clean benchmark known to be absent from pre-training data, the estimated $\\gamma=0.05$ (CI $[-0.4, 0.5]$), confirming that \\contamcal{} does not hallucinate contamination effects where none exist.\n514\t\n515\t\\paragraph{Expected regret in model selection.}\n516\tTable~\\ref{tab:regret} evaluates the practical consequence of calibration via expected regret in top-5 model selection, using a held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (not calibrated scores, to avoid circularity). \\contamcal{} reduces selection regret by 33\\% (12\\% $\\to$ 8\\%), while remove-rescore and DCR increase regret by altering the benchmark's difficulty distribution.\n517\t\n518\t\\begin{table}[t]\n519\t\\centering\n520\t\\caption{Expected regret in top-5 model selection. Ground truth: held-out clean benchmark scores. \\contamcal{} reduces model selection risk by 33\\%, while detection-only methods increase regret.}\n521\t\\label{tab:regret}\n522\t\\small\n523\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n524\t\\toprule\n525\tMethod & Regret & vs.\\ Raw \\\\\n526\t\\midrule\n527\tRaw (uncalibrated) & 12\\% & --- \\\\\n528\t\\contamcal{} (calibrated) & 8\\% & $-33\\%$ \\\\\n529\tRemove-rescore & 15\\% & $+25\\%$ (worse) \\\\\n530\tDCR fuzzy      & 14\\% & $+17\\%$ (worse) \\\\\n531\t\\bottomrule\n532\t\\end{tabular}\n533\t\\end{table}\n534\t\n535\t\\subsection{Multi-Family Validation}\n536\t\\label{sec:multifamily}\n537\t\n538\tTo test whether contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property or a model family artifact, we replicate the controlled SFT protocol on Llama-3.2-1B.\n539\t\n540\t\\paragraph{Cross-family $\\gamma$ consistency.}\n541\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_family} shows that per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ directions are consistent across model families (cross-family correlation $r=0.88$): ARC is consistently negative, GSM8K consistently positive/weak, and MMLU consistently negative. This supports the interpretation that contamination susceptibility is primarily determined by benchmark structure (item format, knowledge domain) rather than model architecture.\n542\t\n543\t\\begin{table}[t]\n544\t\\centering\n545\t\\caption{Cross-family $\\gamma_k$ comparison. Effect directions are consistent across Qwen2.5 and Llama-3.2, supporting the interpretation of contamination susceptibility as a benchmark property.}\n546\t\\label{tab:cross_family}\n547\t\\small\n548\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n549\t\\toprule\n550\tBenchmark & Qwen-0.5B & Qwen-1.5B & Llama-1B & Direction \\\\\n551\t\\midrule\n552\tARC   & $-1.46$ & $-0.62$ & $-1.10$ & All negative \\\\\n553\tGSM8K & $+0.19$ & $+3.77$ & $+0.15$ & All positive/weak \\\\\n554\tMMLU  & $-0.85$ & n.s.    & $-0.70$ & Both negative \\\\\n555\t\\bottomrule\n556\t\\end{tabular}\n557\t\\end{table}\n558\t\n559\tSignal behavior is also consistent: Llama-1B shows S1-dominant detection (AUC=0.96) with S2 (0.50) and S4 (0.48) failing at this scale, matching the Qwen2.5-0.5B pattern. The identifiability boundary shifts by $<$5\\% across families.\n560\t\n561\t\\subsection{72B Observational Study}\n562\t\\label{sec:72b}\n563\t\n564\tWe apply \\contamcal{} to Qwen2.5-72B in inference-only mode (no SFT injection) to demonstrate ecological validity at frontier scale.\n565\t\n566\t\\begin{table}[t]\n567\t\\centering\n568\t\\caption{Qwen2.5-72B observational profile. Signal quality is highest at this scale (S2 AUC=0.88). GSM8K shows significant $\\gamma$, consistent with known pre-training data overlap.}\n569\t\\label{tab:72b}\n570\t\\small\n571\t\\begin{tabular}{lccrl}\n572\t\\toprule\n573\tBenchmark & Accuracy & Est.\\ Exposure & $\\gamma$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n574\t\\midrule\n575\tMMLU  & 0.86 & 0.35 & $+0.40$ & $[-0.15, 0.95]$ \\\\\n576\tGSM8K & 0.93 & 0.60 & $+1.20$ & $[0.25, 2.15]$ \\\\\n577\tARC   & 0.96 & 0.25 & $-0.15$ & $[-0.80, 0.50]$ \\\\\n578\t\\bottomrule\n579\t\\end{tabular}\n580\t\\end{table}\n581\t\n582\tTable~\\ref{tab:72b} reports the observational profile. Signal quality reaches its peak at 72B (S2 AUC=0.88, S4 AUC=0.78). GSM8K shows significant contamination effect ($\\gamma=1.2$, CI $[0.25, 2.15]$), consistent with the ecological expectation that Qwen2.5's pre-training corpus likely contains GSM8K-like data. MMLU and ARC effects are not significant, consistent with the model's saturated performance on these benchmarks.\n583\t\n584\t\\subsection{Closed-Weight 2-Signal Mode}\n585\t\\label{sec:closed_weight}\n586\t\n587\tFor closed-weight models lacking logit/embedding access, \\contamcal{} can operate in a degraded 2-signal mode using only ConStat (S3) and Self-Critique (S4).\n588\t\n589\t\\begin{table}[t]\n590\t\\centering\n591\t\\caption{4-signal vs.\\ 2-signal degradation (3B, ARC). Removing embedding and perplexity signals causes substantial degradation across all metrics.}\n592\t\\label{tab:2signal}\n593\t\\small\n594\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n595\t\\toprule\n596\tMetric & 4-Signal & 2-Signal & Gap \\\\\n597\t\\midrule\n598\tRRF AUC     & 0.993 & 0.620 & $-37\\%$ \\\\\n599\tMAE         & 0.035 & 0.085 & $+143\\%$ \\\\\n600\tCoverage 95\\% & 69\\% & 45\\%  & $-24$pp \\\\\n601\t$\\gamma$ CI width & 1.68 & 3.50 & $+108\\%$ \\\\\n602\t\\bottomrule\n603\t\\end{tabular}\n604\t\\end{table}\n605\t\n606\tTable~\\ref{tab:2signal} quantifies the degradation: RRF AUC drops from 0.993 to 0.620, MAE increases from 0.035 to 0.085, and $\\gamma$ CI width more than doubles. This honestly characterizes the cost of restricted model access. The 2-signal mode provides directional guidance but insufficient precision for reliable calibration, motivating the development of stronger closed-weight detection signals as future work.\n607\t\n608\t\\subsection{QC Ablation}\n609\t\\label{sec:qc}\n610\t\n611\tTo assess whether paraphrase quality confounds our results, we compare full-paraphrase SFT ($d_{100}$) against QC-pass-only SFT using the 7,547/16,697 items that passed automated quality checks.\n612\t\n613\t\\begin{table}[t]\n614\t\\centering\n615\t\\caption{QC-only vs.\\ full $d_{100}$ SFT (0.5B). Differences are small ($\\leq$4pp), confirming that paraphrase noise does not substantially confound IRT estimates.}\n616\t\\label{tab:qc_ablation}\n617\t\\small\n618\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n619\t\\toprule\n620\tBenchmark & Full $d_{100}$ & QC-Only $d_{100}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n621\t\\midrule\n622\tMMLU  & 0.233 & 0.260 & $+2.7$pp \\\\\n623\tARC   & 0.294 & 0.254 & $-4.0$pp \\\\\n624\tGSM8K & 0.365 & 0.370 & $+0.5$pp \\\\\n625\t\\bottomrule\n626\t\\end{tabular}\n627\t\\end{table}\n628\t\n629\tTable~\\ref{tab:qc_ablation} shows small differences ($\\leq$4pp), with IRT $\\gamma$ shifting by only 0.15 between conditions. The dominant behavior at 0.5B/$d_{100}$ is catastrophic forgetting regardless of paraphrase quality, because SFT displaces original knowledge even with noisy paraphrases. This supports using the full paraphrase set for primary results.\n630\t\n631\t%=============================================================================\n632\t\\section{Discussion}\n633\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n634\t%=============================================================================\n635\t\n636\t\\paragraph{Benchmark-dependent calibration.}\n637\tOur central finding is that contamination effects are not uniformly inflationary. ARC exhibits forgetting ($\\gamma<0$) while GSM8K shows inflation ($\\gamma>0$), and these directions are consistent across model families ($r=0.88$). This motivates per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$: a global $\\gamma$ is not significant because opposing effects cancel out (Table~\\ref{tab:joint_ablation}). \\contamcal{} is most effective when the contamination effect exists and is moderate (ARC); on benchmarks where the effect is negligible (MMLU at 1.5B+) or extreme (GSM8K $+39$pp at 3B), the framework appropriately widens uncertainty intervals.\n638\t\n639\t\\paragraph{Signal characterization insights.}\n640\tThe multi-scale signal characterization reveals four distinct behavioral patterns: (1) S1 (embedding) is robust across scales but regime-sensitive (degrades under RL); (2) S2 (Min-K++) is scale-dependent with directional reversal at small scales; (3) S3 (ConStat) correlates with effect magnitude rather than scale; (4) S4 (Self-Critique) shows weak scale-dependent recovery. The Min-K++ reversal is a cautionary finding for the contamination detection community: at small model scales, perplexity-based signals can systematically mislead, producing false negatives that reverse into false positives.\n641\t\n642\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n643\t\n644\t\\emph{Coverage gap.} Our 95\\% CI coverage reaches 69\\% on ARC (best case) and 44\\% on GSM8K, falling short of nominal coverage. SBC diagnostics attribute this partially to local independence violations inherent in evaluating a single model across all items~\\citep{mislevy1986}, cross-scale $\\theta$ bias, and the inadequacy of additive $\\gamma$ for extreme effects (GSM8K). The ME model partially addresses noise propagation ($+9.5$pp coverage improvement) but does not resolve structural violations.\n645\t\n646\t\\emph{Model family scope.} Primary results use the Qwen2.5 family for controlled experimental consistency. Cross-family validation on Llama-3.2-1B confirms directional consistency but represents a single additional data point at one scale.\n647\t\n648\t\\emph{SFT vs.\\ real contamination.} Our controlled contamination uses SFT on paraphrased items, which may not perfectly replicate pre-training contamination. The RL observational study provides ecological validity but lacks ground truth for quantitative assessment.\n649\t\n650\t\\emph{Unidimensional ability.} We model $\\theta$ as unidimensional, which is a simplification for multi-domain benchmarks like MMLU~\\citep{reckase2009multidimensional, burnell2023multidimensional}.\n651\t\n652\t\\emph{Closed-weight models.} The 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique) degrades substantially (MAE: $0.035 \\to 0.085$), limiting applicability to proprietary models.\n653\t\n654\t%=============================================================================\n655\t\\section{Conclusion}\n656\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n657\t%=============================================================================\n658\t\n659\tWe presented \\contamcal{}, a framework that bridges contamination detection and benchmark calibration via contamination-augmented IRT. Our characterization of contamination effects across four model scales and four benchmarks reveals that contamination is benchmark-dependent (forgetting vs.\\ inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic forgetting at 0.5B vs.\\ near-immunity at 7B), and that detection signals exhibit method-specific reliability with the previously unreported phenomenon of Min-K++ directional reversal. Fisher Information analysis provides theoretical identifiability bounds that complement our empirical validation. While coverage gaps and structural model limitations remain, \\contamcal{} represents a step toward principled uncertainty quantification in contaminated evaluation settings.\n660\t\n661\tFuture work includes developing stronger closed-weight detection signals (data attribution methods such as TracIn~\\citep{pruthi2020tracin} and TRAK~\\citep{park2023trak}), extending to item-level heterogeneous $\\gamma_i$ for benchmarks with extreme effects, multidimensional IRT for multi-domain benchmarks, and large-scale multi-family validation.\n662\t\n663\t%=============================================================================\n664\t% References\n665\t%=============================================================================\n666\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n667\t\n668\t\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n669\t\n670\t\\bibitem[Angelopoulos and Bates(2023)]{angelopoulos2023conformal}\n671\tA.~N. Angelopoulos and S.~Bates.\n672\t\\newblock Conformal prediction: A gentle introduction.\n673\t\\newblock \\emph{Foundations and Trends in Machine Learning}, 16(4):494--591, 2023.\n674\t\n675\t\\bibitem[Burnell et al.(2023)]{burnell2023multidimensional}\n676\tR.~Burnell, J.~Burden, and L.~A.~Waller.\n677\t\\newblock Revealing the structure of language model capabilities with multidimensional item response theory.\n678\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2305.13184}, 2023.\n679\t\n680\t\\bibitem[Carlini et al.(2022)]{carlini2022extracting}\n681\tN.~Carlini, D.~Ippolito, M.~Jagielski, K.~Lee, F.~Tramer, and C.~Zhang.\n682\t\\newblock Quantifying memorization across neural language models.\n683\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2022.\n684\t\n685\t\\bibitem[Cormack et al.(2009)]{cormack2009reciprocal}\n686\tG.~V. Cormack, C.~L.~A. Clarke, and S.~B\\\"{u}ttcher.\n687\t\\newblock Reciprocal rank fusion outperforms condorcet and individual rank learning methods.\n688\t\\newblock In \\emph{SIGIR}, 2009.\n689\t\n690\t\\bibitem[De~Boeck and Wilson(2004)]{deboeck2004explanatory}\n691\tP.~De~Boeck and M.~Wilson.\n692\t\\newblock \\emph{Explanatory Item Response Models: A Generalized Linear and Nonlinear Approach}.\n693\t\\newblock Springer, 2004.\n694\t\n695\t\\bibitem[DeKoninck et al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\n696\tJ.~DeKoninck, M.~Mertens, and T.~Guns.\n697\t\\newblock ConStat: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n698\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2024.\n699\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013Ct5PjcQGkavBaXRDvPfXt","session_id":"2e2bc174-5249-4ea4-a7dc-2e60f9f53d62","uuid":"41e7b54a-74eb-4590-84d3-e72290e8b90e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:30:58.817Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019HuG3ZxjT5EzXbRhXohSoa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:intro}\n3\t\n4\tLarge language model (LLM) evaluation increasingly relies on standardized benchmarks to compare models and track progress, yet mounting evidence reveals that these benchmarks are systematically compromised by training data contamination \\citep{chang2025benchmarking}. \\citet{yang2026rl} demonstrate that rephrased benchmark items appearing in training corpora inflate MMLU accuracy by 8--15 percentage points while evading standard $n$-gram decontamination, and that reinforcement learning (RL) post-training with GRPO further amplifies contamination-driven gains while simultaneously concealing the detection signals that would reveal them. Dose-response analyses confirm a monotonic relationship between contamination exposure and score inflation, with effects persisting across model scales \\citep{dong2026dose}. Even minor perturbations to benchmark item surface forms---which should not affect genuinely capable models---produce substantial accuracy drops, questioning the validity of reported scores \\citep{wang2025fragility}. Together, these findings point to a reliability crisis in LLM evaluation: reported benchmark scores increasingly conflate memorization with generalizable capability \\citep{rao2026silicon}.\n5\t\n6\tSeveral methods have been developed to detect benchmark contamination. Min-K\\%++ \\citep{duan2025minkpp} and Time Travel \\citep{oren2024proving} perform membership inference against pre-training corpora; ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} measures performance gaps between original and paraphrased items; Self-Critique \\citep{wust2025selfcritique} identifies memorization through entropy collapse in model self-evaluations; and KDS \\citep{xu2025kds} uses knowledge distillation signatures to flag leaked items. While these methods can detect contamination, none produces calibrated benchmark scores that answer the central evaluation question: \\emph{what would this model's accuracy be had it never seen the test data?} On the psychometric side, item response theory (IRT) has been increasingly applied to LLM evaluation. PSN-IRT \\citep{gu2026psnirt} models item difficulty and discrimination but does not account for contamination. \\citet{polo2026growing} validate IRT at the scale of 400+ models yet treat all items as equally trustworthy. ATLAS \\citep{lyu2025atlas} and \\citet{lalor2024irt} further develop IRT-based evaluation without contamination modeling, and TinyBenchmarks \\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} demonstrates that carefully selected item subsets can approximate full benchmark scores, but again without contamination awareness. HiBayES \\citep{zhuang2025hibayes} provides hierarchical Bayesian uncertainty quantification but ignores item compromise. Inference-time decontamination \\citep{yang2024inference} and targeted probing \\citep{han2026probing} attempt to adjust or detect contamination effects at evaluation time, but neither produces fully calibrated scores with uncertainty quantification. The closest predecessor, DCR \\citep{li2025dcr}, provides adjusted accuracy through fuzzy inference over detection signals but offers no uncertainty bounds and does not address the systematic signal degradation under RL post-training that we document in this work.\n7\t\n8\tThe case for post-hoc calibration rests on a sobering empirical finding: contamination prevention does not work. \\citet{xu2025emperor} systematically evaluate twenty mitigation strategies---including item rephrasing, option shuffling, filtering, and dynamic benchmark construction---and find that none reliably eliminates contamination effects across models and benchmarks. \\citet{zhang2025forget} further demonstrate that standard decontamination procedures fail to fully remove memorized content from model weights. While dynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \\citep{white2024livebench} sidestep contamination by continuously generating new items, and efficient evaluation via item subsampling \\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} can mitigate item-specific biases, neither approach provides calibrated scores for existing contaminated benchmarks. These findings motivate a paradigm shift from contamination \\emph{elimination} to contamination \\emph{adjustment}: rather than attempting to remove a confounder that resists removal, we measure it and statistically control for it. The analogy to observational causal inference is instructive---when randomization is infeasible, valid estimates are obtained not by eliminating confounders but by measuring and adjusting for them under clearly stated assumptions. ContamCal instantiates this ``statistical safety net'' philosophy for benchmark evaluation.\n9\t\n10\tOur approach is grounded in educational measurement's longstanding treatment of item compromise---the leaking of test content to examinees prior to administration. Item compromise IRT models \\citep{eckerly2017,sinharay2017} incorporate exposure indicators as covariates within the item response function, enabling joint estimation of compromise effects alongside standard item and person parameters. Explanatory IRT \\citep{deboeck2004,adams1997} provides the broader theoretical framework for embedding external covariates in item response models. Adapting this psychometric tradition to LLM evaluation requires three non-trivial structural modifications. First, whereas educational IRT assumes independent examinees, LLM evaluation involves individual models producing all responses to a shared item pool, fundamentally altering local independence semantics \\citep{mislevy1986}. Second, binary leakage indicators (compromised vs.\\ uncompromised) must be replaced with continuous exposure scores derived from noisy, imperfect detection signals---necessitating a measurement error model that propagates detection uncertainty into ability estimates. Third, stable examinee ability must give way to regime-conditional ability, as models undergoing supervised fine-tuning (SFT) versus RL post-training exhibit qualitatively different contamination signatures that affect both detection signal reliability and contamination effect magnitude.\n11\t\n12\tWe propose ContamCal, a two-stage post-hoc calibration framework for contamination-adjusted benchmark scoring. In Stage~1 (\\emph{Multi-Signal Provenance Mapping}), four training-free detection signals---embedding-based near-duplicate retrieval, Min-K\\%++ membership inference, ConStat performance gaps, and Self-Critique entropy collapse---are aggregated via reciprocal rank fusion \\citep[RRF;][]{cormack2009} to produce per-item exposure scores. RRF is a deliberate design choice: a learned aggregator would face meta-circularity, as its own training data may be contaminated, precluding unbiased weight estimation. In Stage~2 (\\emph{Contamination-Augmented 4PL IRT}), exposure scores enter a four-parameter logistic IRT model as covariates, with a global contamination effect parameter~$\\gamma$ estimated jointly with item and ability parameters via Bayesian inference. A measurement error model treats the observed exposure scores as noisy proxies for latent contamination status, automatically propagating detection uncertainty into posterior estimates. Calibrated scores are obtained by setting exposure to zero and marginalizing over parameter uncertainty, producing point estimates with 50\\%/80\\%/95\\% Bayesian credible intervals. Models trained via RL receive appropriately wider intervals, reflecting the genuine epistemic uncertainty that arises when detection signals are degraded.\n13\t\n14\tOur contributions are organized hierarchically. At the core: (1)~the ContamCal framework itself---a two-stage pipeline comprising RRF-based provenance mapping and contamination-augmented 4PL IRT with per-benchmark contamination effects and a measurement error model for exposure---that produces calibrated benchmark scores with Bayesian credible intervals; (2)~a signal survival analysis demonstrating that contamination detection signals degrade in a cliff-like, rather than gradual, manner under RL post-training, yielding a signal~$\\times$~regime AUC mapping with practical implications for the detection community; and (3)~dual-track validation through controlled SFT contamination injection across multiple model scales (0.5B--7B) within the Qwen2.5 family (internal validity) combined with an ecological RL case study (external validity). Supporting these core contributions: (4)~a simulation identifiability study grounded in Fisher information analysis, with a 144-condition sweep establishing minimum signal quality and model count thresholds for reliable parameter recovery; and (5)~comprehensive evaluation against seven baselines using five quantitative metrics---calibration MAE, credible interval coverage, interval sharpness, ranking preservation (Kendall's~$\\tau$), and dose-response monotonicity---with bootstrap confidence intervals on all comparisons.\n15\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UVPJeteNu9kkU463iUdhXw","session_id":"2e2bc174-5249-4ea4-a7dc-2e60f9f53d62","uuid":"21ad5863-d059-4243-bfb5-3ed015c3388a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:30:58.411Z"}
